proposals.rationale:
Line 1581 treats a sweep that closes back inside as a reversal vote. The evidence contradicts it:
- the CRT target is first only 39-48%, equal to the null;
- Turtle Soup is at or below geometry;
- daily equity swept highs are retaken more than the null (+5 to +9 pp);
- AMD direction follows the trend regime, not the sweep;
- swept levels predict continuation (sibling E7).
This removes a systematic wrong-way push of D.

result.summary:
The engine already matches or exceeds the conventions of the popular open-source SMC and liquidity scripts, so there is no basic feature to copy:
- body-close BOS/CHoCH with one break per level (LuxAlgo SMC, the smartmoneyconcepts Python package, openalgo);
- strict trade-through sweeps (LuxAlgo's free DOL tool);
- touch, CE and full-fill mitigation with dynamic cropping (TFlab, LuxAlgo);
- iFVG on a close through the whole gap (LuxAlgo IFVG);
- session pivots kept until swept (TFO);
- non-repainting MTF reads.

Large studies of these detectors find no forecasting edge. 44 detectors translated from two popular open-source Pine scripts, tested on 2.2M events across 496 S&P 500 stocks, did not beat a matched random entry (SSRN 7483658). A pre-registered test of a production SMC indicator found zero timing skill (SSRN 7608245). So conventions should be borrowed for robustness and looks, and forecast value must come from geometry plus the engine's own ledger.

The most valuable idea from open-source DOL tools is an honest, sample-sized probability: one shows its draw % as a self-calibrating empirical hit rate per bucket with n. The engine's ledger knows per-family outcomes but never feeds them into the displayed PoT, which another study measured to be off by up to 2x by hour of day. SMC-1 fixes this with the engine's own f_raceAdj pattern, out of sample and Brier-measured.

Open-source DOL tools also commit to a target (lock until reached, stop broken or N bars) and report a balanced state. SMC-2 adds an optional commitment mode with a switch-rate metric, A/B-testable via the ledger.

One engine feature likely has the wrong sign. Near-miss "tests" add to pool magnitude, but evidence (Chung & Bellotti, minute data on 3 assets) shows more prior bounces raise the chance of another bounce, decaying over hours. Under the strict sweep rule that favours levels less likely to be hit. The test also excludes exact touches, contradicting the README (SMC-3).

Drawing anchors are clamped to 4,999 bars because of xloc.bar_index and the 5000-bar max_bars_back cap, which breaks origin-candle anchoring on 1-minute charts. xloc.bar_time plus session-aware time(bars_back=-N) fixes this and allows persistent objects (SMC-4).

Smaller items: slow-ATR geometry and an OB volatility filter, an iFVG significance filter with show-last-N for Clean clutter, and LVNs defined as profile troughs.

Session activity follows local exchange clocks, but the engine defines every session in New York time. In the 2026 US/UK DST-mismatch weeks, the European-open spike in index futures moved from 03:00 to 04:00 ET. The 04:00 bucket beat the 03:00 bucket on 93% of mismatch days, against 21% of winter days and 46% of summer days (mean log-ratio +0.35, 95% CI 0.24-0.46). The gold Shanghai-open spike moved from 20:00 to 21:00 ET, while the US 08:30 and 09:30 spikes did not move. As a result, the engine's Asia range (20:00-00:00 New York) and a Tokyo-local 09:00-13:00 range give a different high or low on 68-87% of US-winter days. On index futures, the full-session (ETH) high is printed outside RTH on 46% of days and the low on 50% (n=216, median gap 0.2 ADR). The engine only knows the chart session's PDH/PDL and has no RTH levels on ETH charts. Three data artefacts produce fake sweeps and fake raid events in D:
- continuous-futures roll jumps (ES Dec-2025 +56.5 pts, 6.2x the median hourly range; TradingView back-adjustment is off by default);
- FX rollover-hour wicks (17:00 NY median wick fraction 0.70 vs 0.51-0.61 for other hours);
- session-open gaps.
Calendar compression does not persist. BTC Saturday range is 0.46 ADR in 2023-25, and Monday takes the weekend range 81.7% of the time (n=416), +9 to +12 pp above weekday ranges at the same distance. Holiday sessions run 0.58-0.76 ADR. Crypto has become a weekday US-hours market: the 13-20 UTC share of activity rose from 35% to 44%. Scheduled releases are asset-specific: Thursday 08:30 claims lift the 08:00 hour 1.33x vs Monday's 1.02x on ES, and FOMC 14:00 bars run 4.9x normal on gold but 1.3x on crude. The literature says release-minute direction carries little information. Published session-directional drifts (overnight drift, pre-FOMC drift) have faded and should not be hard-coded. The top proposals are: market-profile auto-detection with per-asset presets, a DST-correct session clock, RTH/ETH key levels via named sessions ("us_regular"), a rollover/gap artefact guard, a roll handler, a crypto weekend model, measured bars-per-day plus holiday handling, and an asset-aware event calendar. Web research used WebSearch summaries only: the Firecrawl daily credits ran out (refresh in about 20 h), and WebFetch could not reach nber.org or mpra.ub.uni-muenchen.de because they are not in the session's allowed network domains.

