result.summary:
Which liquidity is taken next is almost entirely range geometry. A's gambler's-ruin result (70-81% on 17 previous-candle combinations) reproduces exactly. B's new engine-like races (nearest unswept pools above vs below, 7 datasets, 568-1,430 resolved races each) put geometry at 67-75% and within a few points of calibration, so the grade rises to strong.

A's claim that the ICT daily-bias hard rule is "64-73%" was a scoring artifact: neutral days were counted as down calls. Scored on its own called cases, the rule is right 69-76%, while geometry on the same cases is right 72-80%, ahead by +2.0 to +6.5 pp on 12 of 13 datasets. Where the two disagree, the rule is right only 31-43%. CRT, Turtle Soup, open-relative bias, AMD and the value-area 80% rule likewise add nothing beyond geometry.

The Taylor/Raschke claim ("close near the high → high retested next session 80-90%, close higher ~50%") replicates on 13 series: 75-89% for CLV ≥ 0.8, 79-94% for CLV ≥ 0.9, and a next close up only 43-57%. It is geometry, not direction.

The engine's structure/displacement/raid vote D has no side skill, and choosing sides by it costs 3.8-8.3 pp of first-touch accuracy. Every block-bootstrap interval excludes 0, and its Brier score is 15-25% worse than geometry.

A's redesign stands: D becomes a calibrated P(best buy-side finalist before best sell-side finalist). B2 forced three spec corrections:
- use zero drift, because the engine's drift costs 0.4-6.9% Brier, and use the horizon-conditioned f_raceH ratio, which gains 1-3%;
- gate the learned features, because A's ungated SGD was worse than geometry on 6 of 7 datasets and the learned weights flip sign between markets;
- split scoring into two phases, so the finalists use this bar's draw-free scores.

Geometry-chosen sides raise raw DOL HIT % but cannot raise the distance-matched EDGE, and the README must say so. B3 shows the trader-facing previous-candle readout is calibrated for weekly-from-daily periods but over-predicts "either side reached" on short intraday periods (0.86-0.89 predicted vs 0.79-0.83 observed). P(decided) therefore needs a gated per-timeframe calibration; which-first at the open (73-82%) does not.

Several external figures A cited were downgraded:
- the Strange Labs CRT placebo video could not be found;
- RogueQuant's smash-day result is a single paywalled source;
- the 90.32% PDH/PDL count is a NexusFi NQ 2015 forum tally, not an Elite Trader ES count;
- the edgeful pages report inconsistent figures and could not be opened.

Final set, in priority order: BIAS-1 side ledger with a gated calibrated side model, BIAS-2 Draw Source = Calibrated, BIAS-8 zero-drift races including f_invalidation, BIAS-3 previous-candle module with a CLV base-rate table and calibrated odds in the Clean-table tooltip, BIAS-4 (implemented as ICT-9), BIAS-5 skill in the tooltip with no dimming by default, BIAS-7 alerts, and BIAS-6 (merged into REG-1).

concepts.definition:
Classes of candle t−1 relative to t−2:
- close-through up: H1>H2, L1≥L2, C1>H2;
- sweep-reject up (bear CRT): H1>H2, L1≥L2, C1≤H2;
- the two down-side mirrors;
- inside: H1≤H2 and L1≥L2;
- outside, split by where it closes.
'Taken' means traded through (strict). Equal extremes are not a sweep. Gap opens beyond the extreme count as decided at the open and are excluded from first-touch statistics.

